```java
// do-while expresses "prompt once, repeat if invalid" cleanly
String password;
do {
    System.out.print("Enter password (min 8 chars): ");
    password = scanner.nextLine();
} while (password.length() < 8);
```
Note the required semicolon after `while (...)` — the classic do-while gotcha.

```java
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
int choice;
do {
    System.out.println("Menu: 1=Add, 2=Remove, 3=Exit");
    choice = scanner.nextInt();
    switch (choice) {
        case 1 -> System.out.println("Adding item...");
        case 2 -> System.out.println("Removing item...");
        case 3 -> System.out.println("Exiting...");
        default -> System.out.println("Invalid choice");
    }
} while (choice != 3); // show the menu at least once
```

```java
// Comparable: natural ordering inside the class
record User(String name, int age) implements Comparable<User> {
    @Override
    public int compareTo(User other) {
        return this.name.compareTo(other.name); // sort by name naturally
    }
}

var users = List.of(new User("Alice", 30), new User("Bob", 25), new User("Cara", 35));

var byName = users.stream().sorted().toList();                       // Comparable
var byAge  = users.stream().sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(User::age)).toList(); // Comparator

// Multiple criteria: age descending, then name ascending
var complex = users.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(User::age).reversed().thenComparing(User::name))
    .toList();
```

```java
// Overflow-safe and self-documenting
Comparator<User> byAge = Comparator.comparingInt(User::age);
// equivalently: (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a.age(), b.age());
```
Returning the wrong value (or `0` for different objects) also breaks `TreeSet`/`TreeMap`, which treat `0` as "same key."

```java
public class IntegerCacheDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer a = 100, b = 100;
        System.out.println(a == b);      // true  — same cached object
        System.out.println(a.equals(b)); // true  — correct way

        Integer x = 200, y = 200;
        System.out.println(x == y);      // false — different objects
        System.out.println(x.equals(y)); // true  — correct way

        Integer edge1 = 127, edge2 = 127;
        System.out.println(edge1 == edge2);   // true  — still cached
        Integer over1 = 128, over2 = 128;
        System.out.println(over1 == over2);    // false — beyond the cache
    }
}
```

```java
